                        HERALDS OF THE CEMÍ



    What I was supposed to be finishing was a mural.
    The ground floor of our building used to be the Ballroom, an actual
dance hall, back when the Concourse still had those. Since then it’s
been a laundromat, a church, and a vape shop, in that order, and now it
was a community arts space that wanted augmented-reality murals
projected on its brick so the neighborhood would look like somebody
cared about it. They’d cover my rent, barely. I took the gig for the rent
and stayed for the part where I got to build something.
    The building was the instrument. I’d clamped a contact mic to the
steam riser in my closet, the iron pipe that runs the whole height of the
building like a spine, because a pipe carries every sound in a building
the way a drum carries a hit. I wrote a module to take what the pipe
felt and turn it into light: pitch to color, rhythm to motion, volume to
size. I called it Chismosa, because all it did was listen to the building
and tell me the gossip.
    For three weeks Chismosa had been telling me two things I hadn’t
asked about.
    The first was a shape. A spiral that opened into a triangle, nested in‐
side itself, showing up in the screech of the 4, in the hum of the up‐
stairs heater, in the static between radio stations, the same ratio every
time. I’d lost a whole Saturday staring at it. Pattern is my weakness.
Other people glance at static and keep walking. I fall in.
    The second was uglier, because it wasn’t pretty. An eight-second
loop. Not a song. Something that sat in the same place in every sound
on the block like a thumb on a scale: under a car’s bass, under the bo‐
dega speaker, under some kid’s phone on the stoop. It had no melody.
It just made whatever it rode feel pressed flat. I’d deleted it from the
logs a dozen times as parser garbage, same as the spiral.
    Here’s what I hadn’t put together. The block had been mean lately,
little-mean, not dangerous. A fight outside the bodega on Friday over a
lottery ticket. Two guys I’d known since I was nine, not speaking. The
super’s dog biting a delivery guy, which the super called “a misunder‐
standing.” You could call it a bad month. I called it a bad month.
    Manny texted at 2:41, riding the 6 home from a quince in Mott
Haven.



